WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.440
All right.

00:00.440 --> 00:02.120
So what is SQL partitioning?

00:02.160 --> 00:06.680
It's a technique in order to divide a large table into small pieces.

00:06.680 --> 00:08.840
And each piece we call it a partition.

00:08.960 --> 00:12.840
Well, this sounds like we are dividing one big table into two smaller tables.

00:12.840 --> 00:14.000
But it's not like that.

00:14.000 --> 00:18.040
We are just dividing one table into smaller partitions.

00:18.040 --> 00:21.800
So we're going to see it in the database still as one solid table.

00:21.840 --> 00:25.720
But behind the scenes it is split into multiple partitions.

00:25.720 --> 00:28.080
So now let's go and understand what this means okay.

00:28.120 --> 00:30.720
So now let's say that you have a table at your database.

00:31.120 --> 00:35.840
And over the time this table is getting bigger and bigger where you have like hundreds of millions of

00:35.840 --> 00:36.320
rows.

00:36.360 --> 00:39.000
Now once you have such a big table, what can happen?

00:39.160 --> 00:40.480
Everything can be slow.

00:40.480 --> 00:46.360
So for example, if you are reading the table and the execution plan is doing a full scan of the table,

00:46.360 --> 00:49.920
this can take a long time until all the rows are finished.

00:50.040 --> 00:54.920
And if you decide to make like an index for this table, what's going to happen is you're going to go

00:54.920 --> 01:01.400
and build a very big B-tree index where there are a lot of branches and files and so on.

01:01.400 --> 01:07.320
And having a big index is not always a good thing, because if you do operations like delete rows,

01:07.520 --> 01:12.800
update rows or inserting rows, these operations are going to need long time to process.

01:12.920 --> 01:18.040
So having a big index doesn't mean that you can have a good performance for your Bigtable.

01:18.040 --> 01:23.280
So that means having a Bigtable is problematic because everything is going to be slow.

01:23.400 --> 01:27.680
So now what we can do in order to optimize the performance of this Bigtable?

01:27.840 --> 01:30.360
Well, we can use SQL partitioning.

01:30.680 --> 01:36.560
And in order to do that, we have to understand the behavior and the transactions that are happening

01:36.560 --> 01:37.600
on our table.

01:37.600 --> 01:41.240
And what usually happens is that the table grows over the time.

01:41.240 --> 01:47.480
So you can have like subset of data that belongs to 2023 and another one that is created and updated

01:47.480 --> 01:48.680
in 2024.

01:48.720 --> 01:52.040
And then you have something like more current in 2025.

01:52.080 --> 01:56.680
So that means we have like in our table, all data and as well new data.

01:56.680 --> 02:01.070
And we usually interact with the new data more often than the old data.

02:01.190 --> 02:05.910
So maybe, for example, for 2023 there is like only one read transaction.

02:05.910 --> 02:10.790
And for the data in 2024 we have done like two reads and one writes.

02:10.790 --> 02:13.190
So it is a little bit more than 2023.

02:13.230 --> 02:17.790
But for the new data for the current year, there will be heavy transactions.

02:17.790 --> 02:20.270
So we can have a lot of reads, a lot of writes.

02:20.270 --> 02:22.630
We are updating, inserting, reading.

02:22.630 --> 02:25.430
So a lot of things are going on for the new data.

02:25.430 --> 02:30.550
So that means we are accessing frequently the Bigtable only to interact with the new data.

02:30.550 --> 02:33.110
And we rarely need the old data.

02:33.230 --> 02:39.430
So what we can do, we can go and divide this Bigtable, and we usually divide it by like a date.

02:39.430 --> 02:42.350
So that means we can go and split this table by the year.

02:42.350 --> 02:45.030
And we put each year in one partition.

02:45.030 --> 02:47.310
So at the end we're going to have like three partitions.

02:47.350 --> 02:51.110
And now it's really important to understand that those are three partitions.

02:51.110 --> 02:52.830
They are not three tables.

02:52.830 --> 02:57.070
So that means at the client side the users can see only one table.

02:57.230 --> 03:00.110
But behind the scenes we have like three partitions.

03:00.150 --> 03:04.190
Now let's say that you have a query in order to read the data from 2025.

03:04.230 --> 03:08.190
And now what can happen is SQL will not go and scan all the data from the table.

03:08.190 --> 03:12.350
It's going to go and only target one partition the 2025.

03:12.390 --> 03:18.710
So that means SQL is only scanning the relevant informations, the relevant partition and not the entire

03:18.710 --> 03:19.270
table.

03:19.310 --> 03:21.830
And now we have another benefit of having partitions.

03:21.870 --> 03:24.350
Let's say that you are using a modern database.

03:24.390 --> 03:26.990
And normally they support parallel processing.

03:27.070 --> 03:29.470
So if you have the infrastructure for that what's going to happen.

03:29.470 --> 03:35.070
The database engine can process each partition independently and parallelly.

03:35.150 --> 03:39.710
So whether you are reading or writing data so what's going to happen is SQL is going to process your

03:39.710 --> 03:44.790
queries parallelly, which of course can reduce the overall execution time.

03:44.870 --> 03:50.110
So that means if you have a modern infrastructure like maybe for example the Azure Synapse and so on,

03:50.390 --> 03:55.820
go with the partitions because the partition then could be stored in different servers, and this helps,

03:55.860 --> 04:00.100
of course, the SQL engine to use all the resources at once.

04:00.180 --> 04:07.620
So this means partitions allow scalability and as well parallel processing partitions can make the indexing

04:07.660 --> 04:08.740
more efficient.

04:08.740 --> 04:14.900
So instead of having one very big index for the whole table, if you put an index on a partition table,

04:14.900 --> 04:15.780
what can happen?

04:15.820 --> 04:22.020
Each partition can get its own index, which means the size of the indexes can be smaller.

04:22.020 --> 04:27.900
And of course, this helps a lot with searching for data or as well extending the index itself.

04:27.900 --> 04:33.980
So for example, if you are inserting data to the partition 2025, the SQL will not go and change anything

04:33.980 --> 04:35.220
on the other indexes.

04:35.220 --> 04:39.380
It's going to go and only change the index of the partition 2025.

04:39.380 --> 04:44.700
So as you can see, the power of the partitioning it improves significantly the performance of your

04:44.700 --> 04:48.980
table, whether you are reading or writing data to this big table.

04:48.980 --> 04:52.060
So this is what we mean with partitioning and why we need it.
